// Version history shown in game (newest first). Also update the changelog at the bottom of src/index.html.
const VERSIONS=[['1.14.7','The disturbance meter moved to the top right, under the score, so the top of the screen has three lines on each side: depth, area and deck conditions on the left; materials, clearance and score, and disturbance on the right.'],
  ['1.14.6','The magazine count now sits on its own line above the ammo you carry, instead of on the same line. The weapon and ammo block in the bottom right is narrower, so on small views it stays at the bottom beside the hotbar instead of moving up.'],
  ['1.14.5','The ghost health number now sits on the left above HP instead of at the end of its bar, so the hotbar and its Q icon move back toward the middle and no longer crowd the weapon and ammo lines on smaller views.'],
  ['1.14.4','HUD tidy-up. Along the top, the lift alarm, the breach count, the wave counter and messages each get their own line instead of printing over one another. Above the hotbar, states like creeping, wading, dazed, cloaked and the power-failure countdown stack upward one per line, and hover tips and the V-look box sit above them instead of covering them. A Q icon left of the E and F abilities stands for the item quickbar. On the smallest view with a full hotbar, the weapon and ammo lines move up so they no longer cover the last slots.'],
  ['1.14.3','Plate door rooms can no longer trap you: every loot or vault room behind a plate door has a release button on the inside of the door. Press R beside it and the door opens and stays open for 6 seconds, so blinking or being thrown in past the door is no longer a dead end.'],
  ['1.14.2','Hacking is a little harder: a lock with the right key now fills 30 of the 100 (was 40), and a lock in time with the wrong key 15 (was 20). Hacking skill still adds to both.'],
  ['1.14.1','Hacking: each window now shows a key, W, A, S or D, changing with every lock. Press that key while the signal is inside the window for a full lock; the right timing with the wrong key still counts, for half. The arrow keys work too; R, space and clicking no longer lock. The progress bar is now an old-style block loading bar with a percentage beside it that flips over once a second.'],
  ['1.14','Hacking works differently: instead of a set number of locks, each lock inside the window fills a progress bar by 40, and the hack breaks in at 100. Every time the signal hits either end of the bar and bounces back, the bar drains by 10, so a slow hack can take an extra lock or more. The window still narrows and the signal still speeds up after each lock, and two misses still short the panel out. Hacking skill now adds to every lock as well as widening the window and slowing the signal: up to about 60 a lock, enough to break in with two. Stats shows how much each lock fills.'],
  ['1.13.2','The deck report no longer prints the reward over the grade: the reward now starts just right of the grade letter and wraps onto more lines below it when it is long, moving the run total down to make room.'],
  ['1.13.1','Room corners now show: the inside corner of a wall used to stay black once both walls beside it were lit, so the walls met at their tips around a dark square. A corner now appears as soon as at least two of the walls next to it have been seen and you have seen the floor beside it. Solid rock behind walls stays hidden, as do the walls of a dark room.'],
  ['1.13','Art pass: liquids. Water is now smooth: its edges follow how deep it is instead of the tile grid, with a wet line along the shore, darker damp floor just outside it, and the colour of each sector\'s water. Light ripples through the shallows, slow swells roll in the deep and glints drift across it; water that floods, drains or freezes during a deck updates as it happens. Ice is one frosted patch with a white rim, trapped bubbles, cracks and the odd sparkle. Oil slicks are darker, with oily rainbow colours sliding across them. Toxic spills are soft pools with a bright edge, bubbles that rise and pop, and a little fume. Slime trails are glossy, with a highlight creeping along them. Molten slag glows under drifting dark crust with sparks and heat, and cooled slag is dark glass with cracks that glow and fade. Nothing about how liquids behave has changed.'],
  ['1.12','Art pass: every sector now has its own building materials and lighting. Intake: plated panels and tread plate, pipework walls, warm sodium lamps hanging over each room. Pump Hall: tread plate and mesh catwalks, riveted bulkheads, caged lamps on the walls throwing light down, one or two flickering. Sorting Floor: poured concrete with painted lane lines, concrete walls with conduit, long fluorescent strips that now and then buzz out. Cold Store: ribbed mats and frosted tiles, white subway-tile walls, square cold-white ceiling panels. Brine Works: duckboards, corroded walls crusted with salt, sparse teal lamps that pulse as they fail. Relay Core: perforated floor plates, walls of equipment racks with blinking lights, violet status strips along the wall bases. Each room picks its own floor and each stretch of wall its own finish, the same on the same seed. Rooms are brighter in the pools of their lights and a little dimmer between them; ceiling lights hang above you as you walk under them. The lights are only for looks: they do not change how far you or the creatures can see. On dark decks and after a breaker is thrown they are dead, with the odd one flickering weakly.'],
  ['1.11','Art pass: every armament now has its own look in your hands and on the floor. The sidearm, submachine gun, scattergun, nailer, bolt driver, ray gun, flamethrower, throwing knives, bow, spear, box cutter, crowbar, bat, cable whip, chainsaw, super soaker, riot shield, liquid tank, industrial blower and stun gun each have their own shape and colour and show how they work: guns kick back with a muzzle flash when they fire, the bolt driver\'s bolt flies, the ray gun glows as it charges, the bow draws, the spear thrusts, the whip lashes out, the chainsaw shakes and throws sparks, the soaker sprays, the shield lifts when you block and the tank shows how full it is. Melee is held in your right hand and an off-hand gun forward in your left; with your right hand free you hold a gun in both. Weapons lying on the floor show their own drawing instead of one generic icon, and guards and armed husks carry the same drawings.'],
  ['1.10','Art pass, part 4: every item on the floor is redrawn as a small object lit from the top left, sitting on a soft pool in the colour of its kind (amber ammo, blue materials, green medical, orange food, violet keys and data, white weapons and gear), with a glint that sweeps across now and then. Barrels, crates, chests, lockers, hack panels, vending machines, levers, breakers, workbenches, salvagers, medstations, freezers, coin dispensers, arcade cabinets, lift cameras, floor hatches and wall fans are redrawn too, and still show whether they are used, opened, broken, sold out, spinning, lit or powered down; screens and lamps glow softly. Other furniture gets the same soft shadows. New option: Options > Sprite outlines (off by default) draws a thin line in its own colour around you, creatures, people, items and interactables, so they stand out on busy floors. Hidden hatches, trap plates and cloaked figures are never outlined.'],
  ['1.09','Art pass, part 3: the crew are redrawn from above, each busy with something of their own: the scrap trader rummages in a pack of salvage bigger than they are, the quartermaster stands arms crossed between crates of kit, the cook stirs a steaming pot, Subject Nine sways with sparking wires still taped on, the demolition tech sorts charges by touch, the locksmith\'s picks glint as the hands work, the KR-7 android\'s red eye pulses through its open casing, the mech pilot sits in the hatch of a dead loader, the inspector writes in a notebook, and the bone gambler sits cross-legged, hood up, throwing bones that tumble and settle. Security guards share the survey tech\'s body in blue with a cap, holding whatever they carry; their head turns to look around and the sight cone follows it, and on dark decks a gunman\'s flashlight shows through the darkness. Riot guards wear darker armour, shoulder plates and a gas mask. Floors and walls are repainted with bevelled plates, deeper grates and lit wall caps in every sector\'s colours. Anomalies are new: an unstable rift pulses, pulls light in and tears the picture around it now and then, and on electrical decks it crackles with arcs instead.'],
  ['1.08','Art pass, part 2: every creature is repainted. Instead of outlined circles they are clusters of small dabs in four tones of their own colour, lit from one side (from the top left on lit decks, from your lamp on dark ones), each with its own movement: husks sway and drag a foot, crawlers smear when they dash, a spitter\'s sac swells and glows before it fires, brutes heave, bog frogs leap with their real hops, wasps trail pollen, ghosts are drifting motes of light, slugs leave a glistening trail, duct spiders step and twitch, chargers scrape and kick up dust, and stalkers heave with their move. On dark decks anything that glows (spitter sacs, spider eyes, arc snake sparks, drone lights, ghosts, stalker eyes) shows through the darkness before you see the creature. Every tell and state you knew is still there. Security guards are redrawn in the next part.'],
  ['1.07','Art pass, part 1: you are now drawn as a survey tech seen from above, in an orange suit with a white helmet, a helmet lamp that points where you aim, and a sample pack. Your feet step as you walk, your arms hold whatever you carry (both hands on two-handed weapons, a drawn bowstring, a gun at the ready) and swing with your melee hits. Light falls on you from the top left on lit decks and from your lamp on dark ones. Hurt, adrenaline and night optics still tint you.'],
  ['1.06.5','Spider webs cling: walking all the way through a web tears it and fills half your sticky meter, so a second web or a slime patch soon after can glue you down.'],
  ['1.06.4','Crates are less slippery: every push starts them a little slower and they stop sooner. A full shove slides one about 6 tiles instead of 8. Barrels are unchanged.'],
  ['1.06.3','Stats rows regrouped again: Shove and Shove push sit under Melee dmg, then Fire rate, Aim spread, Gun dmg, Gunfire noise and Ammo per pickup. The stat tip now goes away when the pointer leaves the rows (W/S brings a row back from the keyboard).'],
  ['1.06.2','The Food tab is folded into Craft. Craft now has four views along its top: Workbench, Food (what was the Pouch: eat, active buffs, food level), Cook and Herbalism. A and D move between them; Q and E still change tabs, and C still opens the Workbench.'],
  ['1.06.1','Stats tab rows regrouped: Regeneration, Status build-up and Stun resistance now sit under Damage taken, Backpedal slow under Acceleration, and Gunfire noise under Gun dmg.'],
  ['1.06','Stats tab links and tooltips. Hover a row on the right of Stats (or pick it with W/S after D) and the cores and substats on the left that change it light up in their colour, and a tip explains the stat and lists what affects it: your points with what they add right now ("vitality 3: +12"), your gear, crew file tiers, food and draught buffs and injuries with their share, and the other sources by name. Hover a core or substat (or pick it with A, then W/S) and the rows it feeds are outlined in amber. Four new rows: Status build-up, Skill recharge, Aim spread and Stun resistance, so every substat now shows somewhere. Glances show a decimal. On the Stats tab A and D move between the two columns; Q and E still change tabs. No numbers changed.'],
  ['1.05','Creature traits. From the third deck down, beasts can carry traits that work like your perks, each with a visible mark: armoured (dull plates; takes less damage, glances more, barely staggers), frenzied (red, twitching; attacks and moves faster and hunts you far longer; rare), regenerating (a green pulse; heals when left alone, not while burning), volatile (glowing sacs; bursts into a cloud when it dies), hulking (bigger, tougher, slower, heavy on trap plates), venomous (dripping; its hits poison) and keen (bright eyes; sees and hears further, half blinded by smoke). About 10% of beasts at depth 3, rising to 30% by depth 10; from the third sector some carry two. Traited creatures count for more of a deck, so there are fewer of them, and are worth more points. Crawlers, wasps, drones, plants and ghosts never get traits, nor do hunters and wardens; armed humans can only be armoured, frenzied or keen. V-look names them, the bestiary remembers which traits you have seen on each creature, the Codex has a Traits category, the elite vault danger gives every creature a trait, scenarios lean some ways (a lab outbreak runs volatile and venomous, a crackdown arms its guards with armour and sharp eyes, a cold snap makes freezing bursts), and there is a new work order, Specimen collector. The creature console has a Traits row.'],
  ['1.04','Armed humans. Security guards, riot guards and about a quarter of the husks now carry real equipment, and what they hold is how they fight: sidearms fire deliberate shots, SMGs spray, nailers rattle, crowbars and bats hit harder and slower than bare hands, box cutters quicker. Guards carry a magazine and a spare or two, stop to reload in plain view when they run dry (your moment to push in) and close in to pistol-whip you once they are out. Work vests and plate rigs take damage off and make your hits glance more but slow them down; gas masks shrug off tear gas. Some carry a medkit, a smoke grenade or a flare and use it: patching up after backing off, smoking you out when losing, lighting a dark deck to hunt you. When they go down they drop their weapon (with what is left in the magazine) and often the rest of their kit. Hold V on one to see what it carries. Guards hit about as hard as before. The test range creature console has a Kit row.'],
  ['1.03','Two new tools that share power cells. The industrial blower (off hand; Workbench: 2 pipe, 2 batteries, 3 scrap, comes with 4 cells): aim with right click and hold left click to blow a lane of air about 100 px ahead. It pushes and thins every kind of gas cloud, puts out small fires close in, bends flamethrower fire, shoves barrels, crates, loose items and creatures by size (wasps and drones fly, brutes barely shift, and anything slammed into a wall is hurt), and slows and turns slow shots like globs, seeds and trap darts. Loud, pushes you back a little, one cell per 4 seconds. The stun gun (main hand; Workbench: 2 batteries, pipe, cloth, 2 scrap, comes with 6 cells): hold left click to zap the nearest creature in front about 5 times a second for little damage but fast stun; most creatures reel in under a second, wet ones or ones in water twice as fast with the jolt jumping through the water, drones and cameras take triple damage, zapping in water shocks you a little and an arc can light oil. One cell per 2 seconds. Both are in the test range armory and the Codex.'],
  ['1.02','Tear gas and nerve gas. Tear gas (pale yellow-white) blinds you like smoke and makes you irritated: wider aim, slower stamina recovery and coughing fits that stop you sprinting, with no damage; creatures cannot see through it, and unmasked ones back out of it and shoot wild. Nerve gas (dark violet, lingers) is deadly: steady damage, air drain and a seizing meter that locks you up for a second and a half; it hits creatures too and kills plants and slime creatures. A filter mask halves both. New throwables: the tear gas grenade (pipe, powder, cloth and a bitterwort herb) and the nerve gas grenade (needs a rare nerve agent ampoule from laboratories, archive vaults or crackdown lockers; carry 2 at most). New security: riot guards from depth 6 lob tear gas canisters and see through them, riot drones from depth 7 drop canisters on you and hang back; both far more common in a security crackdown, where alarms can call riot guards too. The test range armory has the new grenades and the creature console lists the new creatures (it now scrolls on small screens).'],
  ['1.01','Sector scenarios: every sector after the first runs under a scenario, announced as you ride in and shown on the sector map and in Run stats. Security crackdown (guards on nearly every deck, drones, cameras, twice the trip scanners, alarms call an extra guard, score x1.3), Lab outbreak (overgrowth and toxic spills, plants, wasps, spiders, slugs and frogs, labs and greenhouses, gas traps, herbs, quarantine checkpoints, x1.2), Flooded section (flooded decks and water, frogs and snakes, washrooms, x1.15), Blackout (dark decks and rooms, breakers already thrown, ghosts and spiders, more light sources in the loot, x1.2) and Cold snap (cold vents and risers, frozen pools, frost on the lift rides, emberleaf, x1.15). Scenarios change what you meet much more than how much. The deck report shows the multiplier beside the deck score, the Codex lists every scenario, and the test lift has a Scenario option (its list now scrolls on small screens). Test decks no longer keep a thrown breaker from the previous test deck.'],
  ['1.00','Security cameras: from depth 3, wall cameras sweep a cone over corridors, doorways and loot rooms. Stay in sight for a second and a half and the cone turns red and the alarm goes off; break sight, cloak or hide in smoke before then and it resets. Shoot or hit one three times to break it, cut the power at a breaker to shut them all down, or hack the panel some are wired to (a failed hack sets off the alarm). Hold V on one to see its state; found cameras show on the station map. The test range has one over the hazards area and the test lift has a Cameras option.'],['0.99','Damage ranges and glancing hits: every hit now rolls its damage inside the weapon\'s range, so a weapon is steady, reliable, swingy or wild (sidearm steady, bat swingy, scattergun pellets wild), with average damage unchanged. A glancing hit, the opposite of a crit, does half damage with little knockback and no stun: about 8% of hits, less with Dexterity and Perception, more against armoured creatures; creature hits can glance off you too. Descriptions, the Workbench, hover tips and Stats show the ranges and chances. Counting work orders now level up from I to IV, each level paying again; the Work orders list shows progress to the next level and your profile keeps your best.'],['0.98','The crowbar can be held to charge a heavier swing, like the bat. The E and F abilities are now two icons beside the hotbar (which moves right to make room), darkening while they recharge. Point at an ability icon or a hotbar slot to see what it does. Hold V and point at anything you can see in the deck, a creature, item, fixture, found trap or hazard, to see what it is.'],['0.97','Hacking is harder to start with and rewards skill more: four locks instead of three, a slightly narrower first window and a fast fourth round. Hacking skill (splicer kit, Surveyor file, Memory) now also skips the last rounds, down to three and then two, and makes each round speed up less.'],['0.96','The name above the hotbar is gone: it repeated the left-click line at the bottom right and covered CREEPING and winded. In psychic and sling mode the bottom-right line now names the selected action.'],['0.95','The Files tab view Upgrades & perks is now just Upgrades, and its upgrades list is called Talents. The pack menu grows on bigger screens: it fills the space between the score row and the health bars, with the same margin on both sides. Its tabs stretch to match. A few overlapping labels in the Food and Stats tabs are tidied up.'],['0.94','The HUD shows how much clearance you have to spend, beside the score. Stun builds faster: every hit adds stun however small it is, so about ten hits in quick succession daze you (fewer if they are heavy), and an explosion right beside you dazes you outright. The crew file details scroll with the mouse wheel when a file has more tiers than fit, and follow the next tier to attune.'],['0.93','Duct spiders are smaller, with thin jointed legs that scuttle as they walk. Smoke now blinds creatures harder than it blinds you: they cannot see you through or inside thick smoke unless they are right beside you, and while it hides you they give up the hunt three times faster.'],['0.92','Four new work orders for investing in crew files: Signed off (attune your first tier), Cross-trained (tiers in 4 different files), Specialist (fully attune a file) and Career track (20 clearance invested). The work orders list now scrolls. Smoke grenades trail smoke along their flight and pour out a much wider screen that lasts longer. Hands and Bones games are shorter: both sides start with 9 instead of 12.'],['0.91','Achievements are now called work orders and live in the Files tab, as its fourth view (Crew files, Upgrades & perks, Skills, Work orders), with point trades beside them. Progress keeps the bestiary and run stats.'],['0.90','Lifts are bigger: the exit lift now sits in the middle of a marked 3x3 floor plate, and R works anywhere on the plate. The freight lift is the same size and now also needs R instead of carrying you off the moment you step on it. Nothing you can use is placed on the plate or the two tiles around it.'],['0.89','The exit lift now needs R: standing on it no longer ends the deck. The same goes for the test lift in the test range and the arcade exit. Nothing else you can use is ever placed on or right beside the lift, so R at the lift always means the lift.'],['0.88','Ghosts move differently: slower and floatier, wandering off line as they come for you instead of homing straight in. When close they shiver, then lunge straight through you, coast on in that direction for a few seconds, and loop back around if you are still near. They still flee when you look straight at them.'],['0.87','Balance: creatures no longer hunt you forever. Once they lose sight of you they keep looking for about 8 seconds (less if they are far off or cannot reach you) and then go back to wandering; seeing you again starts it over. Noise carries half as far through walls. Large decks have 20% fewer creatures. Arenas start with a few creatures and send a wave every 15 seconds: until you kill the mini-boss if there is one, otherwise three waves.'],['0.86','Poison gas traps: a fifth pressure plate trap, its rivets faintly green, that pours poison gas from nozzles around the plate for 5 seconds, filling a room; it re-arms after 20 seconds, creatures set it off too, and a trap panel hack or a power cut stops it. More common on toxic-spill decks and near labs and custodial closets. Toxic floor vents: some floor vents have a sickly green grille and erupt poison instead of steam (about half on toxic-spill decks, one in ten elsewhere). Deck secured: once you have found the exit lift and nothing hostile can reach you, the station map offers a walk back to the lift (G). The time still passes, and anything left on the floor stays there. Ceiling crawlways: medium and large decks can have pairs of ceiling hatches joined by a crawlspace; press R and confirm to climb through, as long as nothing is onto you and you have not just been hurt. The test range has a gas trap, a toxic vent and a crawlway, and the test lift a Secured option.'],['0.85','Express shafts: every sector map has 2 to 4 express shafts, shown as a double amber line with a lightning mark, that run straight past a junction to the one beyond. Riding one costs a battery (2 if it drops you at the exit junction), never stops on the way, and skips that deck with its loot and score, while the hunter still moves a step. They stay hidden like other shafts until you stand at one end or find intel. Vault junctions: one or two junctions per sector, off the direct route and marked with a faint gold signal from the start, are large decks holding an archive vault behind an armoured door. Hack its lock (harder than usual) or, if the lock burns out, blow the door with an explosion. Inside are one or two crew files you have not found, a rare chest and supplies. Every vault deck has a danger: a guardian, an elite garrison, a lockdown or an unstable deck. Cracking one scores a big bonus, and opening vaults in 3 sectors earns the Vault breaker achievement. The test lift has a Vault deck option.'],['0.84','Herbs and herbalism: five wild herbs (redroot, bitterwort, emberleaf, grey sage, ironmoss) grow on overgrown decks against walls and beside blooms, and in greenhouses; a few turn up in lockers, from the galley cook and at camp landings. They go in a herb pouch in the Food tab, and the Forager perk doubles them. A new Herbalism view (A and D switch views) makes poultices (redroot, bitterwort, mending: healing over time, clearing poison and burning, shortening an injury), incense you set down to burn for 30 seconds (grey sage heals you nearby and stops disturbance building; emberleaf keeps a warm patch where nothing freezes) and draughts you drink like food (ironmoss: less damage taken; emberleaf: immune to cold; grey sage: stun resistance and steadier aim; restorative: a quick heal and faster stamina). The test range has a herb stash in the main hall.'],['0.83','Frozen: a full cold meter now freezes you solid for 1.5 seconds. You cannot move, attack or use anything, and melee hits on you do 60% more; mash the movement keys or catch a fire hit to break out sooner, and being ablaze melts the ice at once. Afterwards you cannot freeze again for 3 seconds. Creatures have a cold meter too: cold vents and risers, freezing water and the Frost Matron freeze them for 2 seconds (hunters 1), and frozen creatures take 60% more melee damage and double damage when knocked into a wall. Ghosts and drones never freeze. New cryo grenade (Workbench: battery, pipe, cloth): a freezing cloud for 3 seconds that freezes creatures inside and turns water to ice. Active reload: a marker appears on the reload bar; press R again inside it for an instant reload, or fumble it and wait longer. Dexterity widens the marker. The test range has a cold riser by the west wall.'],['0.82','Plate doors and crates: from depth 2, some decks have a heavy door wired to a floor plate. It stays open only while the plate holds enough weight: you, a creature, a crate or a pile of dropped items. Step off and it grinds shut a second later. They guard small loot rooms, rooms with a chest, or shortcuts, and there is always a crate or loose junk nearby. Crates are pushed like barrels but never burn or explode; wooden ones break under a sledgehammer or a heavy charged hit. Dropped items and crates now set off trap plates too. Spacebar styles: pick what SPACE does in Files > Skills. Shove stays the default; Kick (Brawler file) hits harder and further and can be a flying kick when sprinting; Repulse (Kinetic file) throws everything around you away. The test range has a plate door south of the trap room, and the test lift a Plate door option.'],['0.81','Traps: from depth 2, decks hide pressure plates, mostly in corridors and doorways. Step on one and it clicks, then fires a spike, flame, dart or falling-debris trap, and re-arms after 6 seconds. Creatures that walk onto a plate set it off too, so you can lure them in; flying creatures, ghosts and a floating player do not. With 3 Perception plates glint when you are close, and the sonar pulse and signal scanner mark nearby plates. Some traps are wired to a trap control panel: hack it to lock them down, but a failed hack sets them all off. Cutting the deck power stops flame and dart traps. Fix: arena decks no longer keep the previous deck\'s hack panels and vending machines. The test lift has a Traps option, and the test range has a trap room.'],['0.80','Run seeds: every run has a seed that recreates the same station: sector maps, decks, loot, chest contents and lift events. Fights and luck in the moment still vary. Run setup has a Seed option: random (still recorded), daily (the same for everyone all day) or one you type in, case and spaces ignored. The seed is shown in Run stats and when you die, and saved runs keep it. Typed and daily runs are marked as seeded on the death screen and in the best score. The test lift has a seed field to rebuild a deck.'],['0.79.1','Fixes: a sector map could rarely come out as a short straight line of junctions instead of a spread-out map; it is now always a full map. Arcades with several cabinets, at lift stops and in arcade rooms, no longer repeat a game.'],['0.79','Decks now come in three sizes: small (tight and crowded), medium (as before) and large (sprawling, with more of everything). A sector\'s exit deck and arena decks are often large. Known junctions on the sector map show small or large decks, and the station map scales to fit. Creature pathfinding is spread over several frames so large decks stay smooth. The test lift has a deck size option.'],['0.78','Runs save automatically on the route map between decks, with Continue on the title. Run setup lets you start in any sector you have reached and add challenge modifiers unlocked by achievements and hunter kills (each +25% score); New game still starts in one click. Behind-the-scenes performance tweaks for busy decks.'],['0.77.6','Files tab: perks, injuries and upgrades now share one Upgrades & perks view, in a single scrolling list with details on the right.'],['0.77.4','A janitor\'s closet behind a door at the far end of the arcade hall: locker, wash basin, tool bench and a cot by a bookshelf, lit only by a small lamp.'],['0.77.3','Arcade floor labels are smaller and centred under each cabinet, the restroom has a door, and its sink and mirror are one wall-mounted unit.'],['0.77.1','The arcade gets banks of booth seating along the bottom, the exit lift in its own room on the right, and a short hall above it with a water fountain and a restroom.'],['0.77','Super soaker (main hand) and liquid tank (off hand): fill the tank with R from water, oil or sludge you stand in or from your flask, then spray streams that soak, oil or poison creatures and leave pools. Water puts out fires and cools slag; oiled creatures burn twice as hard. Sensitive file tier 10: Spirit ward, so ghosts cannot touch you.'],['0.76.2','People can also turn up inside fitting rooms behind their doors: a cook in the cafeteria, Subject Nine in the medbay or lab, a quartermaster in the locker room, anyone in the arcade.'],['0.76.1','People are no longer out on the open deck: they wait in small rooms behind closed doors (R to open), or at a new camp landing between decks.'],['0.76','Guns have magazines and reload times: R reloads when there is nothing nearby to use, and an empty gun reloads when you fire. Sidearm 10, SMG 30, scattergun 4, nailer 40, bolt driver 1, ray gun 8, flamethrower 60; bigger guns take longer. Bows, knives and the sling never reload.'],['0.75','New creature: the bloom wasp. Small, darting and wary, often in swarms around blooms on overgrown decks. It hovers out of reach, lunges with a sting, then drifts dazed for a second.'],['0.74.1','The Codex has a Systems category explaining how the game works: movement, light, combat, combos, crits, statuses, injuries, food, crafting, files, skills, disturbance, gas, hazards, hacking, security, the lift, the sector map, hunters and more.'],['0.74','Proximity mines. Injuries: lasting debuffs that happen when you take heavy punishment at low health or with a status maxed out, greying out lost max health on your bar; they heal over lift rides or with surgery kits, medstations, stasis pods or the rare ginsu bean (cooked from glowcap fungus). The Perks view lists injuries and only perks from files you have found. New Codex on the title screen covers creatures, perks, items, gear, resources, food, files, sectors, hazards, people, rooms and lift events.'],['0.73','Breaker panels on electrical decks cut the deck power: the live cables die, but so do the lights and trip scanners. Every sector now has 1-2 arena decks, with side rooms and wall segments, and half of the arenas from depth 3 are held by a biome mini-boss.'],['0.72','Hold V to plant your feet and look far ahead with the mouse, seeing further than usual while you stay on screen. Sensitive file tier 9: Astral projection, leaving your defenceless body behind to scout as a ghost through walls for up to 12 seconds.'],['0.71','You arrive on each deck sealed inside the lift cab and open the doors (R) when ready, so no more ambushes on arrival. Enemy numbers now follow a smooth threat budget: dangerous creatures cost more of it, so deeper decks bring a few heavier threats instead of piles of everything, with brutes, guards, rams and graspers capped and ramping up slowly.'],['0.70.1','Gun, bow, sling and knife hits now build combo a little, melee builds it faster. Perfect hits get a crunchy version of their weapon sound. Creatures stuck in walls get freed, and bestiary traits wrap instead of overlapping.'],['0.70','Route map fog: every junction in the sector is shown, but where shafts lead is hidden until you ride them or find intel (route charts, schematics, terminal route logs, the inspector, the intercom). The hunter boss only shows on the map when it is close or has been sighted.'],['0.70','Sector maps: each biome is a spread-out map of decks you can see all at once, with a known exit lift 3-4 jumps away; reaching it moves you to the next biome. Deeper in, or after a lot of disturbance, a biome boss stalks you across the map (the Intake Maw, Pressure Hulk, Sorter, Frost Matron, Brine Octopus or Arc Lord). Land on its deck and you fight it or run for the lift.'],['0.69.2','Sensitive file tier 8: Detect life. For 8 seconds you sense every living thing within a wide radius through walls, shown as pulsing outlines (red hostile, green friendly, amber plants, pale people).'],['0.69.1','Arcade marquees lose the chasing lights along the bottom and gain a soft, slowly pulsing backlight, as if lit from behind.'],['0.69','Skill sets: the action bar panel in Files > Skills now holds every skill set. New Ranger file teaches Sling, a resource ranger bar that slings endless debris, any ammo type, charges, flasks and grenades, with a charged draw whether aiming or not.'],['0.68.1','Guards look like you in steel blue and carry flashlights: a faint cone shows where they are looking, and they only spot you inside it. They also open fire on any other creature they see, and creatures they shoot turn on them, so firefights give their positions away.'],['0.68','Security: biometric trip scanners stretched across corridors trigger a klaxon, red strobes and 3-5 security guards when you cross them. Guards are slow, tough and shoot; from depth 4 a few patrol on their own and radio for backup (another alarm) if they keep you in sight for a few seconds. Guards called by an alarm cannot call more.'],['0.67','Compound bow: a quiet two-handed weapon that shoots bolts. Hold left click to draw for up to 2.5x power (a perfect release pierces and crits more often), or aim with right click and loose arrows at a steady pace.'],['0.66.1','Fire beats plants and slime everywhere: the flamethrower scorches slime off every tile it touches, and wall plants and snarevines next to any fire catch and burn hard.'],['0.66.1','Fire is the answer to growth and goo: plant creatures, slugs and snails take triple damage from fire and burn longer, fire burns blooms away without setting them off, and flames clear slime.'],['0.66','Overgrown decks are thicker: twice the mendblooms and stingblooms, plus two wall-rooted plants: the seedpod bloom fires fans of seeds, and the tripwire vine lays a thorny tendril across the floor that snaps tight and drags you back when you cross it.'],['0.65','New floor hazard: molten slag pools that burn anything touching them, melt dropped items and ignite barrels. Water turns slag into harmless crust with a burst of steam.'],['0.64','New creature: the duct spider, slow and keeping its distance, spits sticky web and now and then strings a web across 1-4 tiles. Webs slow you like deep water and tear after one pass; fire clears them, and floating passes over.'],['0.63','New creature: the bog frog, a loud swimmer that hops in bursts and lashes with its tongue. Creatures can now hurt each other with attacks: spitter globs, frog tongues and ram charges hit whatever is in the way, so you can bait them into each other.'],['0.62.2','A chunky refusal sound when you try to craft, cook, buy, attune or trade without enough. Slime smears away faster: walking through it wears it down, so a few passes clear it.'],['0.62.1','A Perks view in the Files tab lists every perk you have from crew files, with what it does and where it came from, plus the perks still to unlock.'],['0.62','New people on the station: a scrap trader, quartermaster, galley cook, Subject Nine, a demolition tech, a locksmith, the KR-7 android, a mech pilot and an inspector, each with their own services. Make friendly on a person now gets a reply instead of finding no mind.'],['0.61.1','Stops the browser stepping in during play: no more dragging the game image, text selection, middle-click scrolling or browser shortcuts from shift, alt and tab while you are moving and clicking.'],['0.61','Tabs reordered (Pack, Equip, Craft, Food, Stats, Files, Progress); Progress holds the bestiary, run statistics and achievements with tradeable points. Keys sit with the other resources, a disturbance eye shows how stirred up the deck is, the map names the room you are in, things fall into chasms, and the Kinetic file gains Tether to pull items to you. Fixes a crash when hacked supply lockers dropped food.'],['0.60.4','Every arcade cabinet has its own lit marquee above the screen: the title in big lettering over a small animated scene for that game.'],['0.60.3','Pyrite Steps pillars form a subtle checkerboard. Rift Runner gets three warning beams a round: a lane blinks red, then a beam scours it, clearing its walls and taking a life if you are in it.'],['0.60.2','Arcade refresh: Rift Runner has gold shards to grab for score; Crossline is now a reactor-floor crossing with sweeper drones, timed pulse beams and data chips; Pyrite Steps is now a crumbling-pillar crystal hunt with a pursuing ember. High scores pay bonus scrap.'],['0.60.1','Fix: lift ambushes could spawn nothing, letting you walk straight out. Ambushes now open onto a landing with creatures already waiting and more arriving, and the lift stays locked until they are all dead.'],['0.60','Melee combos: every connecting melee attack builds a combo meter (flow at 5 for faster attacks, momentum at 10 for more damage, frenzy at 15 for both). Getting hit breaks it and it drains when you stop landing hits; guns neither build nor break it. Whiffed swings take twice as long to recover. Brawler gains Rhythm, Flurry and Second nature.'],['0.59','Throwing knives: craft them from scrap (your first batch unlocks them as an off-hand weapon). Silent, accurate, 25% crit, and each knife is its own ammo: they land where they stop so you can pick them back up, though some snap on impact.'],['0.58','Critical hits: guns, melee and orbiting debris can crit for double damage. Each weapon has its own aptitude (bolt driver and box cutter highest, scattergun and chainsaw lowest), raised by Mind, Perception and Dexterity, the lucky tag, a new marksman loupe and two file tiers.'],['0.57.1','Food and healing items can be eaten or used straight from Pack > Items: select one and press F, or click its Use now or Eat now button.'],['0.57','Flamethrower (off hand): hold to spray a short stream of fire that sets creatures burning, lights oil and floors, heats barrels and bursts plants. Runs on a new fuel ammo, craftable from powder and cloth.'],['0.56','Charge attacks for the bat, whip and spear: tap to attack as before, or hold to wind up to 2.5x damage. Release just as the charge fills for a perfect hit with extra damage, knockback and a stun. A full spear charge lunges you forward.'],['0.55.4','Dash works with Float switched on: tapping SHIFT dashes first, with a short trail, then you settle into floating as you keep holding it, the same way dash leads into sprint.'],['0.55.3','Medkits are kept when you are already at full health, and can be used from a quick slot. They go into two new recipes: the Trauma kit (big heal, clears poison, burns and shock) and the Regen shot (2 hp a second for 25 seconds).'],['0.55.2','Cable whip: a main-hand lash with long, thin reach and low damage that builds stun fast. A few cracks in a row leave a creature reeling.'],['0.55.1','The Skills view scrolls: the movement and ability lists stay in their own area with a scroll bar, and the description has a fixed space underneath, so nothing overlaps.'],['0.55','Craftable molotov (burning oil spill), gas grenade (poison cloud) and smoke grenade (smoke screen). Standing inside a cloud now shrouds your vision, most of all in smoke.'],['0.54.3','The spear is longer, held off to one side and jabs forward with a straight thrust hitbox. The chainsaw is held steady, shakes while it runs and throws the odd spark. Broken fans now blow barrels and loose pickups along their gust.'],['0.54.2','Gas clouds are clumps of several drifting puffs instead of perfect circles, and you are only affected where a puff actually is.'],['0.54.1','Broken fans blow gas clouds along their gust, and fans now often come paired with a steam riser beside them or a vent in their path.'],['0.54','Gas clouds: steam risers and vents leave steam clouds that grow the longer they spew and wet you, new noxious pipes on toxic decks leave poison clouds, and fires and explosions give off smoke that uses up a new air meter, also drained by deep water.'],['0.53.1','Sensitive file tier 7: Immobilize pins every creature in a circle in place for 3.5 seconds; they can still be pushed, slammed and pulled around.'],['0.53','Psychic powers split into two classified files: Sensitive (subterfuge and movement: Make friendly, Float, Blink, Quiet mind) and Kinetic (force and damage: push, the new Orbiting debris, gravity well, the new Unravel debuff, pyrokinesis, Shrapnel and Concussive). Both share the Psych action bar.'],['0.52','Submachine gun (sidearm rounds) and chainsaw (runs on power cells). New low-gravity decks: floaty movement, long knockback slides and barrels that drift at a touch. Severe disturbance can now also change the deck: low gravity, flooding, anomalies or dead lights.'],['0.51.2','Barrels sitting or rolling in fire heat up and ignite after about a second, then blow on their usual fuse.'],['0.51.1','Night shift file gains two tiers that slow the disturbance meter: Low profile (20% slower) and Duct rat (another 20%, and failed hacks count half).'],['0.51','Barrels are physics objects: shots, shoves, tackles, the sledgehammer, explosions and psychic push and gravity wells send them rolling. They bounce off walls, knock into each other and creatures, crack on hard impacts, and tip into chasms. Walking into one nudges it along.'],['0.50.1','Float costs half the stamina (less again with Perception and Resolve) and glides like ice. New Blink move from the Sensitive file: a short teleport on SHIFT tap with long invulnerability.'],['0.50','Movement skills are switched on or off instead of bound: dash on SHIFT tap then keep running, creep makes normal walking stealthy. New Float move (Sensitive file) hovers over floors and a new chasm hazard. Psychic actions and floating cost stamina, then health.'],['0.49','The game no longer pauses while the pack is open on a deck: creatures keep moving. An Options screen on the title lets you turn pausing back on, and set the view size.'],['0.48.2','An Open pack button on the route map and stop screens, next to the TAB shortcut.'],['0.48.1','Cook view has an only-cookable filter (X or click), and the Pack tab\'s first view is now called Items.'],['0.48','Food experience: eating earns food xp (more for cooked dishes). Each food level heals you and adds a growing chunk of ghost health that soaks damage after armor but never heals back.'],['0.47','The Pack tab splits into Quick items (consumables and tools), Resources (ammo, materials, keys) and Equipment (gear and armaments). New reusable tools for the quick bar: glowstick, sledgehammer and signal scanner.'],['0.46','Core stats: Body, Spirit and Mind, each with three substats. Every file tier trains a substat, and every 3 substat points raise the core. Rare tonics and sigils from big creatures and cleared arenas raise them directly. Shown on the Stats tab.'],['0.45','Classified files, starting with the Sensitive: its Psych skill swaps your item slots for a psychic action bar (push, gravity well, make friendly, pyrokinesis), set up in Files > Skills. Upgrades moved into the Files tab, and Stats has its own tab.'],['0.44','Crew files are found now: you start with Deckhand and Rigger and find the rest around the station. Upgrades are unlocked by file tiers. New files (Brawler, Armorer, Galley cook), linked files that need another file first, and the sensitive can wipe a file for a partial refund.'],['0.43','Crew files: the Skills tab becomes Files. Spend clearance, earned by finishing decks, to attune to crew files for stat gains, active skills, SHIFT moves and perks. Skill bindings live in the same tab.'],['0.42','Arena decks: one big open room with mirrored cover and a sealed lift that only unlocks once every creature is dead. Also selectable on the test range lift.'],['0.41.4','Stops between decks are more common: possible from the first ride, about 60% of rides, and every run gets at least one proper event early. Passive finds now show a notice.'],['0.41.3','Black screen fix for Firefox: the page now declares its text encoding at the very top, and the version notes moved to the end of the file.'],['0.41.2','Fix for a black screen with no sound: the pixel font now loads in the background, so a slow or blocked font server can no longer stop the game from starting.'],['0.41.1','Sturdier start-up: the screen sizes itself even if the page reports no size at first, and any error now shows on screen instead of leaving a black canvas.'],['0.41','Stops between decks: passive finds, text events, optional landings (merchant, gambler, abandoned quarters, arcade) and lift-cab ambushes. Lift camera terminals show the next stop and can flag it or lock it out.'],['0.40','Cooking: raw ingredients (flour, syrup, tubers, spores, stingberries, grubs, brine fillet, frozen stock) and a Cook view on the Food tab. Overgrown decks hold the best raw food in riskier spots, the Cold Store is lined with freezers, and seven dishes can only be cooked.'],['0.39','Rare dampening terminals and a wandering sensitive who can reset, lock or shake off the disturbance, plus Hands and Bones, a roshambo-and-dice wager game.'],['0.38','A hidden disturbance meter from depth 3: exploring, failed hacks and loud noises fill it, and a full meter draws a wave. Each wave drawn is bigger, waves drawn are scored and tracked for the run, and sometimes they follow you to the next deck. Visible on the map.'],['0.37','Wall salvagers: feed them unequipped gear, weapons, consumables or ammo and they grind it into raw materials. Loud.'],['0.36','An Upgrades tab (U) for character upgrades: melee (moved from the workbench), mobility, ranged, items and survival, each with three levels.'],['0.35.1','Overgrown decks: winding vines across the floor, ivy clusters and flowers on the walls, and taller grass around big puddles.'],['0.35','The test range lift opens a deck builder: pick depth, biome, deck type, lighting, hazard, lift objective, waves and haunting, or roll a random deck. Reaching that deck\'s lift brings you back.'],['0.34.1','Temporary cooldown meters for strike, fire, shove and guard, shown with the status meters while they recharge.'],['0.34','Slamming into walls and obstacles hurts: anything knocked hard into them, you or enemies, takes impact damage. Explosions now throw you. Shoving while sprinting is a tackle: a lunge, more force, a little more damage and reach.'],['0.33.1','Flask fixes: it now shows in the Pack, finds a quick slot or tells you where it went, can always be crafted, and extra flasks are carried as spares that refill your hand after a throw.'],['0.33','Unified hotbar: 1-2 armaments and 3+ quick slots, scrollable. With an item selected, left click uses it and right click drops or throws it. Q cycles items, F is a second skill slot, you start with 3 quick slots, and the new flask collects, drinks and throws liquids.'],['0.32','Five new arcade cabinets (Moonrake, Crossline, Obelisk, Pyrite Steps, Brickfall) and an Arcade option on the title screen with every cabinet and a broken coin dispenser.'],['0.31','Walls you are next to show about half their tile. Some large rooms have dead lights. New modules: cafeteria, bathhouse, sportsball court and custodial closet.'],['0.30.7','Title shadows synced to the lights: each light casts its own dithered shadow that moves and fades with it.'],['0.30.6','Title is solid orange. A slow warm light rises up through the letters, shifting their shadow for a sense of descent, and the quick shine now sweeps bottom to top.'],['0.30.5','Title stacked on two lines with heavier two-tone letters, and the shine now sweeps top to bottom like a passing lift light.'],['0.30.4','Simpler title: tall, widely spaced two-tone lettering with a drop shadow and the glint, on a plain dark screen.'],['0.30.3','PC-98 style title screen: dithered gradient sky and lift shaft, banded logo with magenta drop shadow, bevelled windows and an inverted menu bar.'],['0.30.2','A punchier title: bigger lettering with a rust drop shadow, dark outline, bright top edge, a periodic glint and bracketed rules.'],['0.30.1','Guns work as before: hold right click to aim, left click to fire. Clicking an equipment or armament slot opens a picker to swap or remove what is there.'],['0.30','Armament sets with main and off hands (LMB strikes, RMB fires or blocks), fists, crowbar, bat, box cutter, spear and riot shield, blocking guard and passive wards, a shoulders slot, a Pack tab with 7 quick item slots on 3-9.'],['0.29.1','Ice is slippery underfoot without building the slick status. Freezing spreads one tile less. Test range cold vent moved to the bottom-left of the pool.'],['0.29','Freezing vents and risers that ice over water, the cold status, fire sprinklers, wall fans, ghosts on haunted decks, the ray gun, freight lifts, and overgrown decks with mendblooms, stingblooms, snarevines, herbs and forage.'],['0.28','Modules: greenhouse, locker room, washroom, medbay, break room, laboratory and arcade (with a playable game), behind doors you can open, lock or blow. Food with satiety and deck-long buffs, emetic syrup, and the antitox shot.'],['0.27.1','Rare floating flotsam in deep water that you can stand on: no swimming penalties while you are on it.'],['0.27','The grasper: a deep-water creature that lashes out a tentacle, grabs you and drags you into the water. Shoot the tentacle, shove it off or dash free.'],['0.26','Deep water you must swim through (no weapons, stamina drains while moving), the lurker that lives in it, the incoming-waves deck event, and darker entity shadows.'],['0.25.1','Object and creature shadows are now faint, short, soft-edged shading on the floor instead of darkness, and no longer hide anything.'],['0.25','Barrels, chests and the hermit trader cast soft shadows away from you. The floor behind them darkens like the edge of your light, and anything standing in that shadow is hidden.'],['0.24.1','Default aim line is now faint white and reaches half your light radius. The red laser is the new Laser sight accessory, which also tightens and strengthens aimed shots.'],['0.24','Oil spills: the slick status makes movement slippery and speeds up burning, and oil catches fire and spreads across the spill.'],['0.23.2','Fire: small flame tongues that come and go, a glowing flame bed linking neighboring tiles, and bigger flames where fire is densest.'],['0.23.1','Fire drawn as flickering, swaying flame tongues at offset spots, with scorch marks that spill across tile edges.'],['0.23','Cloak skill: 6 seconds unseen by enemies. Attacking or being hit drops it, and shoves from cloak are ambushes.'],['0.22.1','Map: room corners filled in so walls wrap around, and below-deck rooms outlined in a contrasting dashed blue.'],['0.22','Version history, readable from the title screen and kept at the top of the file.'],['0.21.1','Slime trails drawn as offset, curved, puddled streaks instead of a tile grid.'],['0.21','Burn status (ablaze at 100%, put out by water or rolling) and radiation from anomalies.'],['0.20','Sludge slugs, blight slugs and plated snails. Slime trails and the sticky status.'],['0.19','Status meters: poison, shock, stun (dazed at full) and wet. Filter mask, rubber boots, padded jacket.'],['0.18','Test range creature console and arena pen: release any creature, alert or dormant.'],['0.17','Arc snake with electric pulses, the charging ram, and the alarm-sounding watcher drone.'],['0.16','Swimming enemies. Spitters ignore water, and the brine snake is faster in it.'],['0.15','Lift keycards and hold-the-lift defense with breach cores and floor, wall and ceiling spawns.'],['0.14','Live cabling that arcs through water, chain-reacting fuel barrels, and wall steam risers.'],['0.13.1','Softer rounded water edges. Fewer, more varied puddles.'],['0.13','Loose vents, floor hatches, emergency levers, cages, hermit traders and security offices.'],['0.12','Bestiary tab with discovery and kill tracking.'],['0.11','Scoring, deck reports, grades with rewards, and a saved best score.'],['0.10','Vending machines to buy from, hack or kick.'],['0.9','Adjustable view size at the same pixel scale (V).'],['0.8','Skills tab with bindings and a full stats readout.'],['0.7','Rarer special stops, lighting conditions, fog, steam, fire, toxic and anomaly hazards, hackable panels.'],['0.6','Title menu, test range, water depths, biome layouts, branching lift routes, rest, merchant and signal stops.'],['0.5.1','Fix: firing while holding right click to aim.'],['0.5','Movement momentum, stamina sprint, dash and creep, stim shots, traction gear.'],['0.4','Pack screen, melee upgrades, keys, chests, vaults, secret rooms, vision circle, lights, equipment slots, skills.'],['0.3','Right-click aim mode with laser sight and backpedal penalty.'],['0.2','Station map, nailer and bolt driver, more recipes, flares, tile art pass with sector palettes.'],['0.1','First playable: line-of-sight vision, generated decks, four creatures, workbench, shove, pipe charges.']];
const GAME_VERSION=VERSIONS[0][0];
